     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Saturday, January 23, 2016 at 1:52:35 PM UTC-5, Olive Buhule wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><font face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\" color=\"#000000\"></font><span><span style=\"color:windowtext;line-height:115%;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;font-size:10pt\" lang=\"EN\">I am wondering if STAN allows\r\nragged array assignment of matrices. </span></span></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>No.<br> </div></div>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><span><span style=\"color:windowtext;line-height:115%;font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;font-size:10pt\" lang=\"EN\">And if yes, can someone help me with a\r\ncode to do this.<span>  </span></span></span></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>See section 13.2 of the Stan user manual for a relatively simple example, but it is still pretty awful. The new slicing notation helps a bit.<br><br>Ben<br><br><div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"margin:0in 0in 0pt\"><span style=\"font-family:&quot;Courier New&quot;;font-size:10pt\"><font color=\"#000000\"><br></font></span></div><font face=\"Times New Roman\" size=\"3\" color=\"#000000\">\r\n\r\n</font></div></div></div>"
    ]
